; CTR-Native PICA200 vertex shader -- native track renderer, rung 3a STEP 2.
; docs/NATIVE_TRACK_RENDERER_DESIGN.md 4.2/4.3/12b.
;
; Projects model-space track vertices (NativeTrackVert, via the GPU-side
; N3DS_TrackVertex mirror -- n3ds_backend.h) with a REAL perspective camera,
; unlike the packet path's ctr.v.pica, which only remaps already-CPU-projected
; screen-pixel positions through an orthographic matrix. Derivation (verified
; against platform/native_gte_core.c's GTE_RotTransPers, not assumed):
;
;   view.x = (R00*vx + R01*vy + R02*vz)/4096 + TRX   (same shape for y/z)
;   screenX = OFX + H * view.x / view.z
;   screenY = OFY + H * view.y / view.z
;
; A straight port of that would need a real divide per vertex and a SECOND
; matrix multiply to reach the packet path's shared ortho depth axis. Instead:
; qx/qy/qw (uniforms, composed on the CPU once per frame in
; platform/native_track_frame.c from the double-buffered camera snapshot) are
; built so that w = view.z/H falls out of a plain dp4 against raw model-space
; (vx,vy,vz,1), and a*=w*screenX, b*=w*screenY fall out the same way:
;
;   qx = [ R00/4096 + (OFX/H)*R20/4096, ..., TRX + (OFX/H)*TRZ ]
;   qy = [ R10/4096 + (OFY/H)*R20/4096, ..., TRY + (OFY/H)*TRZ ]
;   qw = [ R20/(4096*H), R21/(4096*H), R22/(4096*H), TRZ/H ]
;
; Feeding S=(a,b,w*zOrd,w) through the packet path's OWN ortho matrix
; (n3ds_backend.c's s_projection, passed here as a second uniform of THIS
; program) then yields outpos = (screenX, screenY, zOrd, 1) after the GPU's
; own perspective divide -- exactly the packet path's own mapping, because
; Mtx_OrthoTilt's row3 is (0,0,0,1): Mtx_Ortho builds it that way by
; construction (it never writes anything else into the last row) and the
; 3DS-panel "tilt" on top of it is a pure rotation, which cannot introduce a
; w/translation term into a row that started at (0,0,0,1) -- checked by that
; matrix-algebra argument, NOT by reading citro3d's own Mtx_OrthoTilt source,
; which is not present locally (only headers ship with this devkitPro
; install; libcitro3d.a is prebuilt). So outpos.w comes out equal to S.w = w
; here (not the packet path's constant 1) -- exactly what a real perspective
; divide needs. Stronger evidence than the matrix algebra alone: the PACKET
; path already depends on this exact same property, today, on real hardware
; -- ctr.v.pica sets r0.w = 1 and computes outpos.w = projection[3]-dot-r0
; through the SAME s_projection this shader reuses. If row 3 were anything
; other than (0,0,0,1), every packet-drawn frame would already be
; perspective-distorted, and it is not: the game renders correctly on
; hardware today. The algebraic argument says row 3 CAN'T be anything else;
; this says it ISN'T, empirically, every frame this renderer has ever drawn.
;
; zOrd (depth compose, design 4.3/12b -- the walk's own bucket->ordinal table,
; rung 1, NOT the abandoned "ordinal widening" v1 design section 12 describes
; as falsified): per LEAF, platform/native_track_frame.c projects the leaf's
; static BSP bbox through the SAME view.z axis (no H term) and converts both
; ends to an OT bucket index with retail's own formula
; (DrawLevelOvr1P_GetProjectedOtIndex, game/226/226_00_DrawLevelOvr1P.c:1950-1976:
; otIndex = clamp((maxDepth>>6) + drawOrder, 0, 1020) -- maxDepth being
; retail's SZ3 register, which native_gte_core.c:319-328 confirms IS this
; same view.z axis, clamped to an unsigned 16-bit range, NOT a reciprocal
; depth), looks up each end's emit ordinal via the rung-1 bucket table
; (NativeGpu_GetOtBucketOrdinal), and passes the leaf's own
; (otIndexLo, ordinalLo, ordinalSpan, invIndexSpan) here as a per-draw
; uniform (N3DS_TrackDraw_Leaf). This shader computes its OWN per-vertex
; otIndex (viewZ/64 + this vertex's drawOrder) and lerps it into that leaf's
; ordinal range.
;
; REVIEW FIX (FIX 1 -- the CPU-side range this lerps into is no longer
; box-geometry alone; platform/native_track_frame.c now widens it by this
; leaf's own [drawOrderMin, drawOrderMax] before it ever reaches this
; uniform, specifically so no in-range vertex needs to extrapolate outside
; [0,1] here -- see that file's NativeTrackDraw_ComputeLeafDepthRange for the
; full fix). What used to sit here was WRONG: a comment calling
; out-of-[otIndexLo, otIndexLo+span] extrapolation "the same harmless
; residual the design doc calls out for within-bucket ties" -- it is not a
; tie-break at all. A valid ordinal (0..65535) already saturates this
; shader's vertex-z clip range end to end (zOrdBias below maps it to
; [-32768, 32767], exactly the depth range N3DS_C3DSetProjection's ortho
; matrix clips against); an EXTRAPOLATED ordinal from a wide lerp
; (invIndexSpan is large exactly when a leaf's box-derived range was
; narrow -- one or two OT buckets) pushes outpos.z/outpos.w outside the clip
; volume, and the GPU DISCARDS those triangles outright. The failure mode is
; geometry vanishing off screen, not a coplanar tie resolving the wrong way.
; The fraction clamp just below is belt-and-braces on top of the CPU-side
; range fix, not a substitute for it: if a future change to the range math
; ever narrows it again, clamping here still stops a wide lerp from reaching
; the clip volume, at the cost of flattening depth at the leaf's own
; extremes instead of deleting geometry.
;
; PICA200 has no SUB opcode (verified against picasso's own mnemonic list),
; so every subtraction below is an ADD against a pre-negated constant:
; drawOrderDebias is -128 (undoes the CPU-side +128 bias in
; N3DS_TrackVertex.drawOrder) and depthRange.x arrives PRE-NEGATED from
; N3DS_TrackDraw_Leaf (see that function's own comment for why the negation
; lives there, not in platform/native_track_frame.c).
;
; PICA200 vertex shaders are float24 (~16-bit mantissa, so integers up to
; roughly 131072 round-trip exactly). Ordinals reach 65535 -- checked against
; that bound, not assumed: 65535 < 131072, so every ordinal value this shader
; ever sees is exactly representable, no precision loss from the float
; register file itself.

; Uniforms
.fvec qx           ; composed camera row: dp4(qx, v1) = w*screenX
.fvec qy           ; composed camera row: dp4(qy, v1) = w*screenY
.fvec qw           ; composed camera row: dp4(qw, v1) = w = view.z/H
.fvec projection[4] ; SAME ortho matrix the packet path's ctr.v.pica uses
.fvec hParam        ; x = H (distanceToScreen_PREV), y = selTrueDepth,
                     ; z = selOrdinal (= 1 - y), w unused. y/z are the runtime
                     ; A/B between the ordinal lerp and the true-depth axis
                     ; (docs/PACKET_DEPTH_DESIGN.md) -- see the TRUE DEPTH block
                     ; in main. Both paths are computed and blended rather than
                     ; branched: PICA200 branching on a uniform would need two
                     ; shader variants or a real jump, and a handful of extra
                     ; ALU ops is the cheap way to get a mid-race hardware A/B,
                     ; which is the only way this gets accepted. Collapse to the
                     ; winner once the owner has ruled.
.fvec depthRange     ; (-otIndexLo, ordinalLo, ordinalSpan, invIndexSpan) -- per leaf
; SEMI-TRANSPARENCY (docs/TRANSPARENCY_DESIGN.md 3). x/y = the UV delta, in this
; file's own ENCODED units (2x fixed point -- the CPU doubles the texel delta
; before it gets here, see N3DS_TrackDraw_Leaf), from a rect's primary placement
; to its PASS2 placement. z/w unused.
;
; This is what lets ONE resident vertex buffer address BOTH decoded views of a
; semi-transparent texture. A rect that needs blending is decoded twice at level
; load -- PASS1 (its non-STP texels) into the primary placement and PASS2 (its
; STP texels) into a second one -- and the baked vertex UV points at the primary.
; The blend pass redraws the same indices with this offset set, so the identical
; geometry samples the other view. No second copy of the vertices, and no
; per-vertex attribute: the offset is a property of the RECT, and build-time
; binning makes every blend draw rect-homogeneous precisely so it can be a
; per-draw uniform instead.
;
; ZERO for every opaque draw, and written on EVERY draw rather than only the
; blended ones -- an unset uniform component holds whatever the last program to
; use that physical register left there, which is exactly the class of bug the
; s29 projection-register collision was.
.fvec uvOffset

; Constants
.constf constants(0.0, 1.0, -1.0, 0.0)
.alias  zeros constants.xxxx
.alias  ones  constants.yyyy

; x = -128 (drawOrder debias, additive -- see the header comment on the
; missing SUB opcode), y = 1/64 (viewZ->otIndex quantizer, matches retail's
; >>6), z = 1/255 (color rescale, same convention as ctr.v.pica's
; colorScale), w = -32768 (ordinal->biased-z, matches N3DS_Vertex's own
; z=ordinal-32768 scheme in n3ds_backend.h so both geometry sources share the
; same signed-s16 depth-substrate axis).
.constf trackConst(-128.0, 0.015625, 0.0039215686, -32768.0)
.alias  drawOrderDebias trackConst.xxxx
.alias  otIndexScale    trackConst.yyyy
.alias  colorScale      trackConst.zzzz
.alias  zOrdBias        trackConst.wwww

; TRUE-DEPTH path constants (docs/PACKET_DEPTH_DESIGN.md 3.6).
; x = 1 -- drawOrder tie-break weight, in view.z units. WAS 64 (one OT bucket,
;     the inverse of retail's >>6) with a comment claiming that gave "FINER
;     granularity than retail's own quantiser". It gave exactly retail's
;     granularity, and on an ABSOLUTE depth axis that is a 64-unit shove -- so
;     a ground face with a negative drawOrder computed as up to several hundred
;     units NEARER than its true distance and won the depth test against
;     anything standing on it. On hardware that buried the player kart to the
;     neck: a coherent plane, constant offset, unchanged with distance, which is
;     the signature of an additive per-face term the packet path has no
;     equivalent for. Packets stamp raw sz3 and carry no drawOrder at all
;     (drawOrder is a QuadBlock field; nothing in Vehicle/ has one), so ANY
;     nonzero weight here puts the two sources on parallel-but-offset axes --
;     the exact failure this file's own comment on zOrdFromDepth warns about,
;     four lines down.
;
;     Design 3.6 specifies no tie-break term whatsoever. 1.0 is kept rather than
;     0.0 because the term's real job is ordering coplanar decals against the
;     surface they sit on, and 1 unit still breaks that tie in retail's
;     direction while being far too small to outrank a kart. If road markings
;     shimmer, this is the number to take to 0 -- the owner's call is that
;     shimmer beats hats poking out of the ground.
; y = 65535 -- SZ3's clamp ceiling (native_gte_core.c's Lm_D), applied here so
;     track depth saturates exactly where the packet path's sz3 saturates.
; z = 32767 -- zOrd = 32767 - depth. Derivation: the packet side stamps
;     (0xFFFF - maxSz3) and N3DS_Vertex maps z = value - 32768, so a packet at
;     depth d lands at (65535 - d) - 32768 = 32767 - d. The track must use the
;     identical expression or the two sources sit on parallel-but-offset axes,
;     which looks correct while parked and wrong in motion.
; w = -1 -- negation for the two subtractions (no SUB opcode).
.constf depthConst(1.0, 65535.0, 32767.0, -1.0)
.alias  bucketToDepth  depthConst.xxxx
.alias  depthMax       depthConst.yyyy
.alias  zOrdFromDepth  depthConst.zzzz
.alias  negOne         depthConst.wwww

; Near-band floor. native_gpu.c reserves the top 256 stamps (0xFF00..0xFFFF)
; for 2D/UI content, which has no depth and must sit in front of every world
; stamp, and clamps world content to 0xFEFF. The track is world content, so it
; takes the identical clamp: 0xFEFF - 32768 = 32511 is the nearest zOrd it may
; produce, i.e. depth may not fall below 256. That costs the nearest 256 units
; of view.z, which are inside the near clip plane and never drawn anyway.
; If PKTDEPTH_UI_BAND changes in native_gpu.c, this MUST change with it --
; the two sides only share an axis while these agree.
; z = 0.0 disables the near-plane reject below (owner ruling, see the REVERTED
; block in main). 1000.0 re-enables it.
.constf depthConst2(256.0, 0.0009765625, 0.0, 0.0)
.alias  depthMinWorld depthConst2.xxxx
; Near-plane reject kick (s29). Any positive multiple large enough that a
; violation of ONE view.z unit already pushes zOrd past the clip volume's
; 32767 end: 1 * 1000 does, and the deepest possible violation (viewZ = 0,
; i.e. exactly at the eye) gives 256 * 1000 = 256000, which is still finite
; and exactly representable in float24, so this can never produce a NaN that
; would poison the whole primitive rather than discard it.
.alias  nearKick      depthConst2.zzzz
; 1/1024 = 1/(2 * N3DS_TRACK_ATLAS_DIM). The 2x is not a typo: UVs arrive in
; the packet path's own atlas convention -- 2x fixed point, +1 for the texel
; centre, V pre-flipped -- so the divisor is twice the atlas dimension. See
; native_track_mesh.c's encode comment. Change one, change the other.
.alias  atlasScale    depthConst2.yyyy

; Outputs
.out outpos position
.out outtc0 texcoord0
.out outclr color

; Inputs (regId order must match N3DS_C3DInit's s_trackAttrInfo setup,
; n3ds_backend.c)
.alias inpos v0 ; x, y, z -- model space, raw GTE units (NOT rescaled: qx/qy/qw
                 ; already fold the /4096 R scale, unlike ctr.v.pica's
                 ; already-screen-space input)
.alias intc  v1 ; u, v -- page-space texel coords, unused until rung 3b
.alias inclr v2 ; r, g, b, drawOrder (biased +128; un-biased below)

.proc main
	; v1 = (vx, vy, vz, 1) -- raw model-space position, homogeneous.
	mov r0.xyz, inpos.xyz
	mov r0.w, ones

	; a = w*screenX, b = w*screenY, w = view.z/H. Constant operand first
	; (src1), input/temp register second (src2) -- PICA200's src2 cannot
	; address the constant/uniform bank (verified empirically for ctr.v.pica;
	; same restriction applies here).
	dp4 r1.x, qx, r0
	dp4 r1.y, qy, r0
	dp4 r1.z, qw, r0

	; viewZ = w * H -- recovers retail's own SZ3/maxDepth axis directly (see
	; the header comment; this is R2.v/4096+TRZ, NOT a reciprocal).
	mul r2.x, hParam, r1.z

	; per-vertex otIndex = floor(viewZ/64) + (drawOrder-128), matching
	; DrawLevelOvr1P_GetProjectedOtIndex's own (maxDepth>>6)+drawOrder shape.
	mul r2.y, otIndexScale, r2.x
	flr r2.y, r2.y
	add r2.z, drawOrderDebias, inclr.w
	add r2.w, r2.y, r2.z                ; otIndex_v (both operands temp regs -- order-free)

	; fraction = (otIndex_v - otIndexLo) * invIndexSpan;
	; ordinal = ordinalLo + fraction*ordinalSpan; zOrd = ordinal - 32768.
	; depthRange.x arrives pre-negated (N3DS_TrackDraw_Leaf), so this first
	; add IS the subtraction.
	add r3.x, depthRange.xxxx, r2.w     ; otIndex_v - otIndexLo
	mul r3.y, depthRange.wwww, r3.x     ; fraction

	; REVIEW FIX (FIX 1b, belt-and-braces): clamp fraction to [0,1] before it
	; scales ordinalSpan. The CPU-side range fix (header comment above) is
	; what actually closes the geometry-deletion bug -- every real vertex's
	; otIndex_v now falls inside [otIndexLo, otIndexLo+span] by construction
	; -- but this clamp stays regardless so a future change to the CPU-side
	; range math can never silently re-open it: an out-of-range fraction
	; here degrades to a flattened depth at the leaf's own extreme (clipped
	; to the valid ordinal range) instead of an extrapolated ordinal outside
	; it (clipped away by the GPU). Zero-span leaves are unaffected: when
	; invIndexSpan (depthRange.w) is 0, fraction is already exactly 0 before
	; either clamp, and max(0,0)/min(1,0) both leave it at 0 -- the existing
	; per-leaf CONSTANT-ordinal fallback is preserved exactly. Constant
	; operand first (src1) in both instructions -- same PICA200 restriction
	; as every dp4/mul above.
	max r3.y, zeros, r3.y               ; fraction >= 0
	min r3.y, ones, r3.y                ; fraction <= 1

	mul r3.z, depthRange.zzzz, r3.y     ; fraction * ordinalSpan
	add r3.w, depthRange.yyyy, r3.z     ; ordinal
	add r6.x, zOrdBias, r3.w            ; zOrd_ordinal = ordinal - 32768

	; ---- TRUE DEPTH path (docs/PACKET_DEPTH_DESIGN.md) --------------------
	; Everything above maps this vertex's real depth onto the packet path's
	; QUANTISED ordinal axis, per leaf, by lerping between two recorded
	; ordinals. s28 measured why that cannot be made seam-stable: the true
	; bucket->ordinal curve is not linear, so neighbouring leaves disagree about
	; a shared edge and the disagreement moves with camera micro-motion.
	;
	; This path goes the other way -- it keeps viewZ and lets the PACKETS come
	; to it (they now stamp sz3, which native_gte_core.c confirms is this same
	; view.z axis). No leaf uniform, no lerp, so there is nothing left to
	; disagree about at a shared edge: two vertices at the same world position
	; get the same depth regardless of which leaf drew them. That is a
	; structural property, not a tuning improvement.
	;
	; depth = viewZ + (drawOrder-128)*64, clamped to [0, 65535].
	; r2.z already holds (drawOrder - 128) from the otIndex math above.
	mul r4.y, bucketToDepth, r2.z       ; drawOrder in view.z units
	add r4.z, r2.x, r4.y                ; viewZ + tie-break (both temps, order-free)
	max r4.z, depthMinWorld, r4.z       ; stay out of the 2D near band

	; NOTE (s32): the max() above maps any vertex at or behind the eye
	; (viewZ <= 0) to 256, the NEAREST world depth. Leaf visibility is
	; leaf-granular and retail's per-polygon near-clip was deleted for track
	; content, so a leaf straddling the eye is drawn whole and its behind-eye
	; corner is declared maximally near. Several sink "fixes" that touched this
	; (near-band polarity flip; behind-eye far-kick; additive/multiplicative
	; road depth bias) were all built and owner-falsified in s32 -- see the
	; memory file ctr-3ds-s32-sink-is-road-depth-offset. The sink is NOT a
	; wrong number here (both depth sources are bit-exact vs the GTE); it only
	; appears when the true-depth axis is active (dmodel ON). Left clean.
	min r4.z, depthMax, r4.z
	mul r4.w, negOne, r4.z              ; -depth (no SUB opcode)
	add r6.y, zOrdFromDepth, r4.w       ; zOrd_true = 32767 - depth

	; Blend the two by the uniform selector rather than branching (see hParam).
	; hParam.z is 1-hParam.y, supplied by the CPU so no subtraction is needed.
	mul r6.z, hParam.yyyy, r6.y
	mul r6.w, hParam.zzzz, r6.x
	add r4.x, r6.z, r6.w                ; zOrd

	; ---- NEAR-PLANE REJECT (s29) -- REVERTED, OWNER RULING ----------------
	; DISABLED (nearKick is 0.0 below, so the four instructions are a no-op
	; kept only as the record). Owner rejected the trade on sight and was
	; right to: whole-triangle discard makes track polygons vanish wherever a
	; triangle straddles the near plane, and in a RACING GAME the ground is
	; near-parallel to the view constantly, so that case is not an edge case,
	; it is every frame under the player's own kart. Losing ground under the
	; kart is worse than the occlusion it was meant to fix.
	;
	; PROCESS NOTE: this trade was explained to the owner only AFTER it
	; shipped. It should have been a decision put to him before a line was
	; written -- his standing perf-over-parity ruling covers visual downgrades,
	; NOT holes in the track surface.
	;
	; The behind-camera problem it targeted is REAL and still open (measured:
	; [TRKDEPTH0] reports viewZLo=0 on live frames, so leaves at/behind the eye
	; are drawn whole and the depthMinWorld clamp maps them to the NEAREST
	; world depth). The correct fix is a proper near-plane clip or CPU-side
	; leaf rejection, not a vertex-shader discard. Re-enable only by giving
	; nearKick a non-zero value, and only after the owner has ruled again.
	; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	; MEASURED, not assumed: [TRKDEPTH0] reports viewZLo=0 on real frames --
	; leaves whose near edge sits AT OR BEHIND the eye are drawn whole,
	; because v1 visibility is leaf-granularity and retail's own per-polygon
	; near-clip ("near-clip records") was deleted for track content. The
	; depthMinWorld clamp above then maps every one of those vertices onto
	; 256, i.e. the NEAREST world depth the axis can express -- so geometry
	; that should not be on screen at all outranks karts that genuinely are.
	; That was this renderer's last remaining occlusion bug, isolated with
	; g_dbg_depthTest state 2 (track tests but does not write depth): karts
	; become permanently visible the moment the track stops writing depth.
	;
	; The comment that used to sit on depthMinWorld claimed the clamped range
	; was "inside the near clip plane and never drawn anyway". The log says
	; otherwise, so this rejects instead of clamping.
	;
	; No SUB opcode and no discard in a PICA vertex shader, so this uses the
	; mechanism this shader already documents (see the FIX 1 header comment):
	; z outside the ortho matrix's [-32768, 32767] clip volume makes the GPU
	; DISCARD the triangle. Whole-triangle discard is cruder than retail's
	; true clip -- a triangle straddling the plane vanishes rather than being
	; split -- but it is strictly better than the same triangle covering the
	; screen at maximum nearness, and it matches the owner's standing
	; perf/parity ruling. r7 deliberately, NOT r5: r5 is the S vector below.
	mul r7.x, negOne, r2.x              ; -viewZ
	add r7.y, depthMinWorld, r7.x       ; 256 - viewZ  (> 0 only when too near)
	max r7.y, zeros, r7.y               ; in-range vertices contribute exactly 0
	mul r7.z, nearKick, r7.y
	add r4.x, r4.x, r7.z                ; in range: unchanged. Too near: clipped away.

	; S = (a, b, w*zOrd, w) -- assembled component-by-component: a single mov
	; can only read ONE source register (with swizzle), so gathering four
	; components from three different temp registers takes four movs, not one.
	mul r4.y, r1.z, r4.x                ; w*zOrd (both temp regs, order-free)
	mov r5.x, r1.x                      ; a
	mov r5.y, r1.y                      ; b
	mov r5.z, r4.y                      ; w*zOrd
	mov r5.w, r1.z                      ; w

	; outpos = s_projection * S -- the SAME ortho matrix + row3 argument the
	; header comment makes: outpos.w comes out equal to S.w = w, not 1.
	dp4 outpos.x, projection[0], r5
	dp4 outpos.y, projection[1], r5
	dp4 outpos.z, projection[2], r5
	dp4 outpos.w, projection[3], r5

	; Rung 3b: intc arrives already ENCODED in the packet path's atlas
	; convention -- 2x fixed point, +1 texel-centre bias, V pre-flipped for
	; PICA's bottom-up T axis -- so all this does is normalise by 1/1024
	; (= 1/(2*N3DS_TRACK_ATLAS_DIM)). The encode lives in native_track_mesh.c
	; because that is where the rect placement is known.
	;
	; The flip is NOT optional and does NOT live in the upload: an earlier
	; version of this shader carried a comment claiming it did, skipped it, and
	; reproduced the ten-session texture-quilt bug -- each rect mirrored
	; vertically into its neighbours. native_renderer_3ds.c's own UV write says
	; plainly that the fix is sample-side.
	;
	; Every declared output component must still be written (the s14 hardware
	; boot hang; Azahar does not enforce this), so z/w get explicit zeros.
	; Semi-transparency: shift to the rect's PASS2 placement before normalising.
	; uvOffset is zero on every opaque draw, so this add is a no-op there rather
	; than a second code path. Both operands are non-constant (uniform + input
	; attribute), so src1/src2 order is unconstrained here -- unlike the
	; mul below, whose constant must lead.
	add r8.xy, uvOffset.xy, intc.xy
	mul outtc0.xy, atlasScale, r8.xy
	mov outtc0.zw, zeros

	; outclr: colorScale rescales the raw GPU_UNSIGNED_BYTE fetch back to
	; 0..1 (same convention as ctr.v.pica). Alpha is a constant 1.0 -- inclr.w
	; is NOT alpha here, it already fed drawOrder above.
	mul outclr.xyz, colorScale, inclr.xyz
	mov outclr.w, ones

	end
.end
